"## Why This Question Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to design fault-tolerant Unix log ingestion across mixed formats, archives, and rotation, with checkpointing and deduplication.

## Key Concepts

- Log rotation and gz archives
- Mixed log formats (text and JSON)
- Multi-line event parsing
- Event deduplication using fingerprints
- Persistent state and restart safety

## Code Example

```javascript
// Fingerprint function for deduplication
function fingerprint(hour, message) {
  const normalized = (message || '').toLowerCase().trim();
  const hash = require('crypto').createHash('sha256');
  hash.update(`${hour}:${normalized}`);
  return hash.digest('hex');
}

// Checkpoint persistence
function saveCheckpoint(checkpoint) {
  const tempPath = '/tmp/checkpoint.json';
  fs.writeFileSync(tempPath, JSON.stringify(checkpoint));
  fs.renameSync(tempPath, '/path/to/checkpoints.json');
}
```"

## Why This Is Asked
- Assesses ability to build robust, single-line data pipelines over multi-file logs, including compressed files, without external dependencies. 
- Tests time-window handling, cross-service aggregation, and per-tenant bucketing under rotation. 

## Key Concepts
- Stream processing with standard UNIX tools (find, xargs, zcat, awk). 
- JSON field extraction via regex in awk; resilient to line variations. 
- Time-window filtering using START/END derived from date; cross-file aggregation. 
- Sorting by computed metric (error rate) without extra tools.

## Code Example
```javascript
START=$(date -u -d "-6 hours" +"%Y-%m-%dT%H")
END=$(date -u +"%Y-%m-%dT%H")
find /var/log/cluster -type f \( -name '*.log' -o -name '*.log.gz' \) -print0 | \
  xargs -0 -I{} sh -lc 'case "{}" in *.gz) zcat "{}" ;; *) cat "{}" ;; esac' | \
  awk -v s="$START" -v e="$END" '{
    if (match($0, /"ts":"([^"]+)"/, a)) ts=a[1]; hour=substr(ts,1,13); gsub(/T/," ",hour); hour_key=hour;
    if (match($0, /"tenant":"([^"]+)"/, b)) tenant=b[1];
    if (match($0, /"lvl":"([^"]+)"/, c)) lvl=c[1];
    if (hour_key && tenant && hour_key>=s && hour_key<=e) {
      total[tenant] += 1; if (lvl=="ERROR") errors[tenant] += 1;
    }
  }
  END { for (t in total) { rate = total[t] ? errors[t] / total[t] : 0; printf "%s\t%.6f\n", t, rate } }
``` 

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to include per-hour breakdowns in addition to the overall top 3? 
- How would you handle non-UTC timestamps or malformed JSON lines?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to handle mixed log formats with rotation, per-host aggregation, and time-windowed analysis using standard UNIX tools.

## Key Concepts
- Mixed log formats (standard access logs and JSON lines)
- Handling both .log and .log.gz files
- Graceful handling of unreadable files
- Time parsing and windowing in shell (epoch conversion)
- AWK associative arrays for per-host tallies
- Robust parsing of multi-line error blocks without double-counting

## Code Example
```bash
find /logs/nginx -type f -name '*.log*' -print0 | while IFS= read -r -d '' f; do
  if [[ "$f" == *.gz ]]; then
    zcat "$f" 2>/dev/null || continue
  else
    cat "$f" 2>/dev/null || continue
  fi
done | awk -v cutoff="$(date -d '2 hours ago' +%s)" '
/^ERROR:/ { error_block=1; next }
error_block && /^[[:space:]]/ { next }
error_block { error_block=0 }
{
  if (match($0, /\{"ts": *([0-9]+)/, ts)) {
    timestamp = ts[1]
  } else if (match($0, /^([0-9]{1,3}\.[0-9]{1,3}\.[0-9]{1,3}\.[0-9]{1,3})/, host)) {
    split($0, parts, /\[/)
    if (match(parts[2], /([0-9]{2}\/[A-Za-z]{3}\/[0-9]{4}:[0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2})/, date_match)) {
      cmd = "date -d '\''" date_match[1] "\'' +%s"
      cmd | getline timestamp
      close(cmd)
    }
  }

  if (timestamp && timestamp >= cutoff) {
    if (match($0, /"status": *([45][0-9]{2})/, status)) {
      counts[host[1]]++
    } else if (match($0, /\s([45][0-9]{2})\s/, status)) {
      counts[host[1]]++
    }
  }
}
END {
  for (h in counts) print h ":" counts[h]
}' | sort
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates the ability to handle mixed text and JSON logs, manage log rotation and compression, and perform cross-host aggregation with time-based filtering. It tests regex parsing, time-windowing, deduplication logic, and working within POSIX shell constraints without external dependencies.

## Key Concepts
- POSIX shell constraints with gzipped logs and mixed formats
- Time-window filtering for the last 30 minutes
- Robust user extraction from both plaintext and JSON logs
- Case-insensitive deduplication with per-host aggregation

## Code Example
```bash
for host in $(awk -F'[/=]' '/^\/(logs|var)\/log\// && !/^#/ {print $3}' /etc/hosts 2>/dev/null | sort -u); do
  users=$(find "/logs" -name '*.log*' -mmin -30 2>/dev/null | \
    xargs -I{} sh -c 'zcat -f "{}" 2>/dev/null' | \
    awk -v host="$host" '
      BEGIN {now=systime(); cutoff=now-1800}
      /^[[:space:]]/ {next}
      /^"?[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2} [0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}/ {
        gsub(/[\[\]:]/, "", $1" "$2)
        cmd="date -d \""$1" "$2"\" +%s 2>/dev/null || date -d 0 +%s"
        cmd | getline ts; close(cmd)
        if (ts >= cutoff) {
          if ($0 ~ /ERROR|WARN/) {
            for (i=1; i<=NF; i++) {
              if ($i ~ /^user[=:]/) {
                gsub(/[,:"\047]/, "", $(i+1))
                print tolower($(i+1))
              }
            }
          }
        }
        next
      }
      /^"?\{/ {
        if (match($0, /"ts"[[:space:]]*:[[:space:]]*"([^"]+)"/, t)) {
          cmd="date -d \""t[1]"\" +%s 2>/dev/null || date -d 0 +%s"
          cmd | getline ts; close(cmd)
          if (ts >= cutoff && match($0, /"user"[[:space:]]*:[[:space:]]*"([^"]+)"/, u)) {
            print tolower(u[1])
          }
        }
        next
      }
    ' | sort -u | wc -l)
  echo "$host: $users unique users"
done
```
